    'body' => <<<'HTML'
<p>An insurer’s first estimate is written from what an adjuster could see on the day of the inspection. Once a roofer starts tearing off shingles or a restoration crew opens a wall, the picture often changes. <strong>Insurance claim supplements in Dallas</strong> are how those newly found or originally missed items get added to the claim. Done well, a supplement is not an argument. It is a clear, documented request that shows what was found, why it is covered, and what it costs. Dallas Public Adjuster helps homeowners and property owners across Dallas County prepare supplements that are specific and easy for an insurer to evaluate.</p>

<h2>When a Supplement Is Needed</h2>
<ul>
<li><strong>Hidden damage found during repairs.</strong> Soft or rotted decking under a hail-damaged roof, wet insulation, damaged framing, or water behind cabinets.</li>
<li><strong>Items missed in the original scope.</strong> Gutters, downspouts, vents, flashing, ridge caps, skylights, screens, fences, or siding on an elevation the adjuster did not inspect.</li>
<li><strong>Measurement errors.</strong> Roof squares, pitch, waste factors, or wall and floor areas that were understated.</li>
<li><strong>Pricing gaps.</strong> Unit costs that do not match current Dallas-area labor and material prices.</li>
<li><strong>Code-required work.</strong> Items such as drip edge, underlayment, or ventilation when the local building department requires them for the permitted work.</li>
<li><strong>Overhead and profit.</strong> General contractor overhead and profit on projects that require coordination of several trades.</li>
</ul>

<h2>What Insurers Commonly Miss on Dallas Roofs</h2>
<p>Most Dallas homes have composition shingle roofs over brick veneer, and many older roofs in the Park Cities, Lakewood, and Lake Highlands have steep pitches, multiple layers, or complex valleys. Commercial buildings and apartment complexes usually have flat or low-slope TPO or modified-bitumen roofs. Each type has its own common misses:</p>
<table>
