<figure><img src="/wp-content/uploads/2026/04/Hail-Damage-Claim-Supplements-What-Carriers-Miss-in-Their-Scope-2-1024x683.webp" alt="Two men inspecting a composition shingle roof" width="1024" height="683" loading="lazy" decoding="async"><figcaption>A second roof inspection can identify items left out of the original scope.</figcaption></figure>

<h2>Code Upgrades and Ordinance or Law Coverage</h2>
<p>Re-roofing and most structural repairs inside the City of Dallas generally require a permit from the City of Dallas Development Services Department, and the work must meet the city’s adopted International Residential Code and International Building Code. Suburban cities such as Richardson, Garland, Irving, Plano, and Mesquite have their own permit offices. When the building department requires upgrades the original estimate did not include, ordinance or law coverage may pay for them, up to its limit. That coverage is often a separate, limited part of the policy, so check your declarations page. Our <a href="/local-building-codes/">local building codes</a> page has more background.</p>

[[cta]]

<h2>How to Document a Supplement</h2>
<ol>
<li><strong>Stop and photograph.</strong> When hidden damage appears, have the contractor pause and take wide and close-up photos with a reference for location and scale.</li>
<li><strong>Notify the insurer in writing.</strong> Tell the adjuster what was found and offer a chance to inspect before the area is closed up.</li>
<li><strong>Measure and describe.</strong> Record quantities, such as sheets of decking or linear feet of edge metal, and the reason each item is needed.</li>
<li><strong>Tie each item to the cause.</strong> Explain how the item relates to the covered loss or to a code requirement.</li>
<li><strong>Prepare an updated estimate.</strong> A line-item Xactimate supplement makes it easy to compare against the original.</li>
<li><strong>Attach supporting documents.</strong> Include permits, inspection notes, invoices, and material receipts.</li>
<li><strong>Track depreciation.</strong> Under replacement cost policies, submit proof of completed repairs so recoverable depreciation can be released within any deadline in your policy.</li>
</ol>

<h2>Supplement Mistakes to Avoid</h2>
<ul>
<li><strong>Covering up damage before it is documented.</strong> Once new decking is nailed down or a wall is closed, it is much harder to prove what was there.</li>
<li><strong>Sending a total instead of line items.</strong> A single higher number gives the adjuster nothing specific to approve.</li>
<li><strong>Waiting until the job is finished.</strong> Late supplements can run into policy time limits and invite questions about what was actually found.</li>
<li><strong>Mixing covered and non-covered work.</strong> Upgrades you chose, such as a different roofing product, should be separated from covered repairs.</li>
<li><strong>Losing track of depreciation.</strong> Recoverable depreciation usually requires proof of completed repairs within a stated period.</li>
<li><strong>Relying on phone calls.</strong> Keep supplement requests and responses in writing so the record is clear.</li>
</ul>

<h2>Supplements vs. Disputes</h2>
<p>Most supplements are resolved through documentation and a conversation with the adjuster. If the insurer agrees an item is covered but disagrees on how much it costs, <a href="/services/insurance-appraisal/">insurance appraisal</a> may be available. If the insurer says the item is not covered at all, that is a coverage question, and the steps on our <a href="/services/denied-insurance-claims/">denied insurance claims</a> page may help.</p>

<h2>How We Help With Supplements</h2>
<p>We review the original estimate against the property and your policy, inspect the areas in question, coordinate with your chosen contractor to collect evidence, and prepare and present a documented supplement. We represent the insured only and do not perform repairs on claims we adjust. We cannot guarantee that every item will be approved. Fees are disclosed in a written contract before any work begins; Texas law caps public adjuster compensation at 10% of the insurance settlement. To see whether your claim may need a supplement, request a <a href="/free-claim-review/">Free Claim Review</a>.</p>
